    "case_summary": "A 43-year-old woman of Northern European ancestry, born to nonconsanguineous parents, presented with persistent back and bilateral hip pain. Medical history includes spine fusion at age 12 for cervical scoliosis, hospitalization at age 2 for failure to thrive, and two uncomplicated pregnancies. Family history notable for maternal aunt with hemorrhagic stroke from cerebral aneurysm. Birth measurements: ninth percentile for weight, 58th percentile for length, and fourth percentile for head circumference. Patient had septate uterus leading to hysterectomy at 42 years.\n\nPhysical examination revealed:- Arm span-to-height ratio: 1.07- High-arched palate with crowding- Left corneal clouding- Increased range of motion in elbows, hips, and knees with normal strength- Decreased motion with surgical scarring in lower back- Doughy skin with palpable bilateral piezogenic papules- Pes planus- Untreated umbilical hernia- Moderate myopia (right eye –2.25, left eye –5.25)- Beighton score: 8/9\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Genetic analysis: 92 connective-tissue genes sequence analysis and deletion/duplication testing performedImaging Studies:- Echocardiogram (age 42): Normal aortic measurements, tricuspid aortic valve- CT brain (age 30): Normal- MRA neck, brain, chest, and abdomen (age 43): Unremarkable, no evidence of aneurysms- Spinal imaging: Thoracolumbar spinal scoliosis- Sacroiliac joint imaging: Joint degeneration without inflammatory arthritisImages:- Figure 1: Radiographic findings of thoracolumbar spinal scoliosis and sacroiliac joint degeneration- Figure 2: Four-generation pedigree showing family history of cerebral aneurysm",
